---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import TargetHeartRateCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/TargetHeartRateCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="target-heart-rate-calculator">
  <TargetHeartRateCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the target heart rate calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The target heart rate calculator uses the Karvonen method. It takes the range between your resting
      and your maximum heart rate, the heart rate reserve, and adds a share of it to your resting rate:
      <code>THR = (HRmax − HRrest) × intensity + HRrest</code>. Intensity is a fraction, so 70% is 0.70.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you do not know your maximum heart rate, the calculator estimates it from your age with one of
      three formulas: Fox <code>HRmax = 220 − age</code>, Tanaka <code>HRmax = 208 − 0.7 × age</code> or
      Gellish <code>HRmax = 207 − 0.7 × age</code>. The 10-second count is <code>THR ÷ 6</code>, for a
      pulse check during exercise.
    </p>
    <p>
      The five zones are bands of heart rate reserve: zone 1 is 50 to 60%, zone 2 60 to 70%, zone 3 70 to
      80%, zone 4 80 to 90% and zone 5 90 to 100%.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 35-year-old with a resting heart rate of 62 bpm. Fox gives a maximum of 220 − 35 = 185 bpm, so the
      heart rate reserve is 185 − 62 = 123 bpm. At 70% intensity the target is 123 × 0.70 + 62 = 148.1 bpm,
      about 25 beats in 10 seconds. With Tanaka the maximum is 183.5 bpm and the target 147 bpm. Zone 2 for
      this person runs from 136 to 148 bpm.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Age formulas give the maximum heart rate of an average person; your true maximum can be 10 to 20 bpm
      higher or lower. Beta blockers and some other medicines lower the heart rate, so the zones do not
      show effort well for people who take them. Heat, dehydration, caffeine and poor sleep raise the heart
      rate at the same effort. If you have a heart condition or have not exercised for a long time, ask a
      doctor before hard exercise.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
